    "answer": "### Findings\n\nThe affected path is:\n\n`h1 \u2014 s4 \u2014 s2 \u2014 s1 \u2014 s3 \u2014 s6 \u2014 h5`\n\n- All switches and links on this path are active.\n- `h1 \u2192 h5` ping has **0% loss**, but high latency: **78.9 ms average**.\n- The 5-second TCP test was severely constrained: the sender managed only **131 KB (~0.21 Mbit/s)**, with no receiver payload reported.\n- Nearby paths are healthy:\n  - `h1 \u2192 h3`: **~33.6 Gbit/s**, ~0.7 ms ping\n  - `h7 \u2192 h5`: **~32.3 Gbit/s**, ~0.8 ms ping\n  - `h1 \u2192 h2`: **~40 Gbit/s**\n- Reverse `h5 \u2192 h1` reached **~23.7 Mbit/s**, indicating strong directional asymmetry.\n\n### Localisation\n\nThe clearest fault evidence is on **s2 port 1, transmitting toward s1 port 1**:\n\n- Transmission rate during sampling: **~0.99 Mbit/s**\n- Queue backlog: **6,056 bytes / 2 packets**\n- **363 queue overlimit events in two seconds**\n- No packet errors or interface drops\n\nThe corresponding ports on s1 and s3 showed no backlog or overlimit events.\n\n### Diagnosis\n\nThe cause is most consistent with **egress rate limiting or an impaired queue on `s2:1 \u2192 s1:1`**, likely the Mininet interface `s2-eth1`. This also appears to introduce substantial latency. It is not an h1/h5 endpoint problem or a general link-down condition.\n\nNext, inspect the traffic-control configuration and queue statistics on that interface, for example:\n\n```bash\ntc -s qdisc show dev s2-eth1\ntc class show dev s2-eth1\n```\n\nLook for an unintended ~1 Mbit/s shaper and/or delay configuration.",
